            <ArticleTitle>A randomized controlled trial of intracameral lidocaine during phacoemulsification under topical anesthesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>68-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To test the hypothesis that adjunctive intracameral 1% lidocaine reduces intraoperative pain during phacoemulsification using topical anesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, double-masked, randomized, controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 200 patients undergoing routine phacoemulsification under topical 1% tetracaine were studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomization to 0.5 ml intracameral, unpreserved, epinephrine-free 1% lidocaine or placebo was conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Intraoperative pain was quantified by the patients using a 0-10 visual analog pain scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Intraoperative pain scores (+/- standard deviation) for the lidocaine and control groups were 1.29 +/-1.24 and 1.44 +/- 1.33, respectively (P &gt; 0.35).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In a rigorously double-masked, prospective, randomized, controlled trial there was no significant reduction in intraoperative pain when intracameral 1% lidocaine was used during phacoemulsification under topical anesthesia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
